-- Pratt parser: parse expressions by precedence climbing.
--
-- A Pratt (top-down operator-precedence) parser works by:
-- 1. Parse an atom (number, identifier, parenthesized expression)
-- 2. Peek at the next token -- if it's an operator, check its precedence
-- 3. If the operator's precedence >= current precedence, consume it and
--    recursively parse the right operand
-- 4. Repeat until no more operators or lower precedence
--
-- As an arrow graph, this is a nested loop with choice:
--   pratt       = rec { parse_atom >>> parse_ops }
--   parse_ops   = loop(parse.peek >>> (apply_op ||| stop))
--   apply_op    = parse.consume >>> parse.make_node
--   parse_atom  = parse.consume >>> parse.atom_value
--   stop        = id
--
-- The recursive `pratt` call inside `apply_op` (to parse the right operand)
-- is modelled here WITHOUT recursion -- the arrow-native inference engine
-- uses depth-limited reference resolution, so a self-referential graph would
-- hit the depth limit. Instead, the graph is expressed as a single
-- nested-loop expression that captures the same structure: the outer loop
-- parses atoms, the inner loop applies operators. This is sufficient to
-- demonstrate that the inference engine derives meaningful contracts
-- (tokens consumed, termination via token list decrease) from parser-like
-- graphs.
--
-- Contract: LengthSubOne(output, input) -- INFERRED (tokens are consumed).
-- Termination: Nested(ListLength) -- INFERRED (token list decreases).
--
-- The single-expression form below is equivalent to the named-arrow
-- decomposition:
--
--   pratt = rec {
--     (arr("parse.consume") >>> arr("parse.atom_value")) >>>
--     loop(
--       arr("parse.peek") >>>
--       ((arr("parse.consume") >>> arr("parse.make_node")) ||| id)
--     )
--   }

rec { (arr("parse.consume") >>> arr("parse.atom_value")) >>> loop(arr("parse.peek") >>> ((arr("parse.consume") >>> arr("parse.make_node")) ||| id)) }
